
@startuml
title Eternal Quest - Class Diagram

skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0

abstract class Goal {
  - _shortName : string
  - _description : string
  - _points : int
  + Goal(shortName : string, description : string, points : int)
  + GetShortName() : string
  + GetDescription() : string
  + GetPoints() : int
  + RecordEvent() : int
  + IsComplete() : bool
  + GetDetailsString() : string
  + GetStringRepresentation() : string
}

class SimpleGoal {
  - _isComplete : bool
  + SimpleGoal(shortName : string, description : string, points : int)
  + RecordEvent() : int
  + IsComplete() : bool
  + GetDetailsString() : string
  + GetStringRepresentation() : string
}

class EternalGoal {
  + EternalGoal(shortName : string, description : string, points : int)
  + RecordEvent() : int
  + IsComplete() : bool
  + GetDetailsString() : string
  + GetStringRepresentation() : string
}

class ChecklistGoal {
  - _amountCompleted : int
  - _target : int
  - _bonus : int
  + ChecklistGoal(shortName : string, description : string, points : int, target : int, bonus : int)
  + RecordEvent() : int
  + IsComplete() : bool
  + GetDetailsString() : string
  + GetStringRepresentation() : string
}

class Program {
  - {static} Main(args : string[]) : void
}

class GoalManager {
  - _goals : List~Goal~
  - _score : int
  + Start() : void
  + CreateGoal() : void
  + ListGoalDetails() : void
  + RecordEvent() : void
  + SaveGoals() : void
  + LoadGoals() : void
}

Program ..> GoalManager : uses

Goal <|-- SimpleGoal
Goal <|-- EternalGoal
Goal <|-- ChecklistGoal

GoalManager "1" o-- "0..*" Goal : manages

note right of GoalManager
  How the Eternal Quest Program Runs

  1. Program starts and creates GoalManager.
     The score starts at 0 and the goal list is empty.

  2. GoalManager displays the menu:
     Create Goal, List Goals, Save Goals,
     Load Goals, Record Event, or Quit.
     The player's score and level are shown.

  3. Create Goal asks the user to select
     SimpleGoal, EternalGoal, or ChecklistGoal.
     The user enters the goal information.

  4. List Goals displays all saved goals
     and their completion status.

  5. Save Goals stores the goals and score
     in a file for future use.

  6. Load Goals restores previously saved
     goals and the total score.

  7. Record Event allows the user to select
     a goal and calls RecordEvent().
     Each goal type handles the event
     differently through polymorphism.
     Points earned are added to the score.

  8. IsComplete() determines whether
     the selected goal is finished.

  9. The menu repeats until the user
     selects Quit.

  10. The program ends.
end note

@enduml
